#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192311 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1acdfb7b-9e3d-5475-bb07-d5e2944294f2" :knowledge-point/title "Using an Implicitly Defined Curve to Determine a Second Coordinate"}] :question/id #uuid "4b721d58-c019-5874-a194-0564792d6fe9" :question/math-academy-id "e-10900" :question/problem "Consider the curve $x^3-\\ln y=1$. Given that $x=x(t)$, $y=y(t)$, and that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}=2$ on the curve at the point where $x=1$, find the value of $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}$ at that point." :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{3} - \\ln y &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dt}(x^{3} - \\ln y) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(1) \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dt}(x^{3}) - \\frac{d}{dt}(\\ln y) &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{3}) - \\frac{dy}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dy}(\\ln y) &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot 3x^{2} - \\frac{dy}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{1}{y} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}=2\n$$\n\nand $x=1$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 - \\frac{dy}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{1}{y} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $y$, but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=1$ into the equation of the curve.\n\nAt the point where $x=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{3} - \\ln y &= 1 \\\\\n1 - \\ln y &= 1 \\\\\n\\ln y &= 0 \\\\\ny &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $y=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 - \\frac{dy}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{1}{1} &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192312 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "522fc628-3f81-566b-b7ce-10c75b626c0e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Related Rate From an Implicitly Defined Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "399c1168-d80e-5969-9c93-0ab34f7576a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-10912" :question/problem "Consider the following ellipse:\n\n$\\dfrac{x^2}{2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{4}=1$\n\nGiven that $x=x(t)$, $y=y(t)$, and that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}=\\sqrt{2}$ on the curve at the point $(1,\\sqrt2)$, find the value of $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}$ at that point." :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{y^{2}}{4} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{y^{2}}{4}) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(1) \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{x^{2}}{2}) + \\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{y^{2}}{4}) &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{x^{2}}{2}) + \\frac{dy}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dy}(\\frac{y^{2}}{4}) &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot (x) + \\frac{dy}{dt} \\cdot (\\frac{y}{2}) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}=\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\n$x=1$, and\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt2\n$$\n\nand solve\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nWe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot (1) + \\sqrt{2} \\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192325 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05dd2261-9fbc-5f6c-8c75-357e656b6e6d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Equation of a Conic Section to Determine a Second Coordinate"}] :question/id #uuid "18eca494-51ed-5f9c-95cc-6c9dc8efd474" :question/math-academy-id "e-10963" :question/problem "Consider the section of the hyperbola\n$\\dfrac{x^2}{3}-\\dfrac{y^2}{2}=1$\nthat lies in the first quadrant. Given that $x=x(t)$, $y=y(t)$, and that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}=-2$ on the hyperbola at the point where $x=3$, find the value of $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}$ at this point." :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2}}{3} - \\frac{y^{2}}{2} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{x^{2}}{3} - \\frac{y^{2}}{2}) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(1) \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{x^{2}}{3}) - \\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{y^{2}}{2}) &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{x^{2}}{3}) - \\frac{dy}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dy}(\\frac{y^{2}}{2}) &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot (\\frac{2x}{3}) - \\frac{dy}{dt} \\cdot y &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}=-2\n$$\n\nand $x=3$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot (\\frac{2 \\cdot 3}{3}) - (-2) \\cdot y &= 0 \\\\\n2\\frac{dx}{dt} + 2y &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= -y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $y$, but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=3$ into the equation of the hyperbola.\n\nAt the point where $x=3$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2}}{3} - \\frac{y^{2}}{2} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{3^{2}}{3} - \\frac{y^{2}}{2} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{2} &= 2 \\\\\ny^{2} &= 4 \\\\\ny &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used $y > 0$ since we're only interested in the first quadrant.\n\nFinally, substituting $y= 2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= -y \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193162 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1acdfb7b-9e3d-5475-bb07-d5e2944294f2" :knowledge-point/title "Using an Implicitly Defined Curve to Determine a Second Coordinate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c20eee6-79ed-5858-8156-3d478e04843e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191761} {:answer/id #uuid "d31c039a-7bae-5253-a877-80100e0fd2e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191762} {:answer/id #uuid "17130fe0-8dae-57cf-a1b3-9b1f39793574" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191763} {:answer/id #uuid "49b028a8-5c21-5b98-a1e2-43829eadcadd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191764} {:answer/id #uuid "525fc477-40fd-55ef-ad0e-0451addadaac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17130fe0-8dae-57cf-a1b3-9b1f39793574" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191763} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e5c3519-9f4c-5247-8566-9945fcff7ecd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191760}] :question/id #uuid "9a72cc8b-f0dc-5848-bf1a-1efa5e385ea0" :question/math-academy-id "q-48800" :question/problem "Consider the curve $x + \\ln y = 4$. Given that $x = x(t)$, $y = y(t)$, and that $\\frac{dx}{dt} =-1$ on the curve at the point where $x = 4$, find the value of $\\frac{dy}{dt}$ at that point."}]}
